Here’s a step-by-step guide to implement this, including instructions for creating a visually appealing UserForm:

Step 1: Open the VBA Editor

	1.	Open your Excel workbook.
	2.	Press Alt + F11 to open the VBA Editor.

Step 2: Create a New UserForm

	1.	In the Project Explorer (on the left pane of the VBA Editor), right-click on the project name (your workbook’s name) and select Insert > UserForm.
	2.	A blank UserForm will appear.

Step 3: Design the UserForm

	1.	Resize the Form:
	•	Click and drag the corners of the UserForm to make it large enough to display instructions and buttons (e.g., ~300px width and ~200px height).
	2.	Add Labels:
	•	From the Toolbox (if it’s not visible, go to View > Toolbox), drag two Label controls onto the form.
	•	Set their captions:
	•	Label 1: “Select Holdings File:”
	•	Label 2: “Select Relationships File:”
	•	Arrange them neatly at the top.
	3.	Add Buttons for Browsing Files:
	•	Drag two CommandButton controls onto the form and place them next to each label.
	•	Set their captions:
	•	Button 1: “Browse…”
	•	Button 2: “Browse…”
	•	Rename the buttons in the properties window (press F4 if the properties window isn’t visible):
	•	Button 1: btnHoldings
	•	Button 2: btnRelationships.
	4.	Add a Submit Button:
	•	Drag another CommandButton below the previous buttons.
	•	Set its caption to “Submit”.
	•	Rename it: btnSubmit.
	5.	Enhance Aesthetics:
	•	Select the UserForm itself (click anywhere blank on the form).
	•	In the Properties window:
	•	Set BackColor to a light pastel shade (e.g., light blue or gray).
	•	Set Caption to “File Import Wizard”.
	•	For each label:
	•	Set Font to a larger size (e.g., 11) and a modern style (like Calibri or Segoe UI).
	•	For buttons:
	•	Set Font to match the labels and adjust size (e.g., 10).
	•	Add a company logo or an image (optional):
	•	Use the Image control from the toolbox to add an image to the form.

Step 4: Add Code for File Selection

	1.	Double-click the “Browse…” button for Holdings (btnHoldings) to open the code editor for that button.
	2.	Paste the following code:

Private Sub btnHoldings_Click()
    Dim fd As FileDialog
    Set fd = Application.FileDialog(msoFileDialogFilePicker)
    With fd
        .Title = "Select Holdings File"
        .Filters.Add "Excel Files", "*.xls; *.xlsx; *.xlsm", 1
        If .Show = -1 Then
            MsgBox "Holdings file selected: " & .SelectedItems(1), vbInformation
            HoldingsFile = .SelectedItems(1)
        End If
    End With
End Sub


	3.	Repeat for the “Browse…” button for Relationships (btnRelationships) and replace "Holdings File" with "Relationships File".

Step 5: Add Code for Submit Button

	1.	Double-click the “Submit” button (btnSubmit) to open its code editor.
	2.	Paste the following:

Private Sub btnSubmit_Click()
    If HoldingsFile = "" Or RelationshipsFile = "" Then
        MsgBox "Please select both files before submitting.", vbExclamation
        Exit Sub
    End If

    ' Import sheets
    Call ImportSheets(HoldingsFile)
    Call ImportSheets(RelationshipsFile)

    MsgBox "Sheets imported successfully!", vbInformation
    Unload Me
End Sub

Step 6: Add Sheet Import Logic

	1.	In the Project Explorer, double-click ThisWorkbook under your project.
	2.	Add this helper function:

Private Sub ImportSheets(filePath As String)
    Dim wbSource As Workbook
    Dim ws As Worksheet
    
    Application.ScreenUpdating = False
    Set wbSource = Workbooks.Open(filePath)
    
    For Each ws In wbSource.Sheets
        ws.Copy After:=ThisWorkbook.Sheets(ThisWorkbook.Sheets.Count)
    Next ws
    
    wbSource.Close SaveChanges:=False
    Application.ScreenUpdating = True
End Sub

Step 7: Display UserForm on Workbook Open

	1.	In the Project Explorer, double-click ThisWorkbook.
	2.	Add the following code:

Private Sub Workbook_Open()
    UserForm1.Show
End Sub

Step 8: Save and Test

	1.	Save the workbook as a Macro-Enabled Workbook (*.xlsm).
	2.	Close and reopen the workbook to test.
	•	The UserForm should automatically appear.
	•	You should be able to browse and select files, and the sheets from those files should be imported.

Additional Tips for Aesthetic Improvements

	1.	Add Icons to Buttons:
	•	Use small icons for the “Browse” and “Submit” buttons.
	•	Add an Image control to the form and set the Picture property.
	2.	Alignment:
	•	Align all controls using the “Format” menu in the VBA Editor (Format > Align).
	3.	Progress Feedback:
	•	Add a progress bar (optional) or a message indicating successful completion.
	4.	Colors:
	•	Use consistent colors for buttons, background, and text to match your theme.
	5.	Logo or Branding:
	•	Add your company’s logo or a header to the form.

